WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.020
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents episode 47 for May 13th, 2026, your daily AI briefing.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.480
Let's see what happened in the AI world today. And trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:24.840
Real-time multi-model agents now run full duplex perception and generation without external VAD or frozen states.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:35.720
Thinking Machines Lab just dropped a research preview of TML Interaction Dash Small, a native multi-model model built for continuous collaboration.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:48.980
The system ingests synchronized 200 millisecond chunks of audio, video, and text through parallel streams and keeps a full duplex exchange going without separate voice activity detection.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:01:01.760
A real-time component handles the live interaction, while an asynchronous background model manages sustained reasoning and tool use, both sharing the full conversation context.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.380
This setup removes the turn-based freeze that most current agents still carry.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:24.780
The release also includes revision, a patch selector that trims visual token usage by roughly 46% on computer use trajectories while adding three points of success rate on OS World, Webtail Bench, and AgentNet Bench.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:35.160
Builders can already test the interaction prototype in Google AI Studio and drop the revision selector into their own agent runs this week.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:45.820
The core advance is the dual model split that keeps perception fluid while the background process runs longer reasoning without locking the conversation.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:55.680
Teams working on desktop or voice first agents will want to watch how that split behaves as context length and tool depth grow.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.360
Now let's move through the model updates that landed alongside it.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:10.600
Hebatron is the first open-weight Hebrew adapted Nematron 3M of experts model.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:21.980
It activates only 3 billion parameters inside a 30 billion parameter backbone and hits 73.8% on Hebrew reasoning benchmarks.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:32.600
The model supports 65,000 tokens of native context after a three-phase curriculum and two million bilingual fine-tuning examples.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:47.760
It outperforms dicta L3-24b thinking and stays competitive with Gemma 327b IT on TS8000, HE and Israeli trivia.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:59.540
Developers targeting Semitic language tasks now have a high throughput option that runs roughly nine times faster than dense equivalence at inference time.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.200
Rad takes a different route to capability distillation.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:16.220
It uses an uncertainty-aware contextual banded to allocate a fixed token budget across interdependent capabilities instead of treating each one in isolation.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:24.180
The method improves downstream utility while reducing harmful spillover compared with standard distillation baselines.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:31.320
The public GitHub implementation is already available for teams running the next round of model compression.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:43.300
Big camera hidden state coupling connects two frozen language models through a lightweight neural interface on intermediate activations rather than serialized text.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:57.180
On arithmetic and logic tasks the approach lifts accuracy from 36% to 96% and delivers 1.7 times gains on zebra logic when paired with a Z3 solver.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:06.200
The added interface uses roughly 1% extra parameters and learns a selective protocol directly from task loss.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:13.240
That gives tool augmented systems a route around output vocabulary bottlenecks.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:25.960
Turning to agent and tool releases, revision trains multimodal models on trajectories that drop redundant visual patches across consecutive screenshots while preserving spatial structure.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:43.420
When applied to quen 2.5 VL7B with five history frames, it cuts token count by about 46% on average and raises success rate three on OS world, web tail bench and agent net bench.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:50.840
Agent builders can keep longer visual histories under the same context budget by integrating the patch selector code.

